# Day 372 - Count Good Triplets

## Core Idea

The problem asks us to find every combination of three indices:

```
i < j < k
and verify whether the three pairs satisfy their respective limits.
The three required conditions are:
|arr[i] - arr[j]| <= a
|arr[j] - arr[k]| <= b
|arr[i] - arr[k]| <= c
If all three are satisfied, the triplet is good.
Thought Process
At first, this problem may look like it needs some clever optimization.
But the constraints make brute force completely reasonable.
Since we need three indices, three nested loops naturally fit the problem.
The important part is setting the starting points correctly:
j = i + 1
k = j + 1
This automatically guarantees:
i < j < k
So we don't need an additional condition to verify the index ordering.
Checking the Triplet
For every (i, j, k), calculate:
Math.abs(arr[i] - arr[j])
Math.abs(arr[j] - arr[k])
Math.abs(arr[i] - arr[k])
Then compare each result with:
a
b
c
respectively.
Only when all three comparisons are true do we increment the answer.
Why Brute Force Works
There are at most O(n³) possible triplets.
Because the constraints are small, checking every triplet is fast enough.
This is a good example of why we should always look at the constraints before trying to optimize a solution.
Pattern
for i
    for j = i + 1
        for k = j + 1

            if all three conditions are satisfied
                count++
Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n³)
Space Complexity: O(1)
Lesson Learned
Don't optimize before you need to.
A simple solution that comfortably fits the constraints is often better than introducing unnecessary complexity.
For this problem, three loops + three conditions = clean solution. 😌
